  "markdown": "<figure type=\"ILLUSTRATION\">\n\n<description>This is a black and white engraving depicting a historical scene, likely William Penn's Treaty with the Lenape. In the center, a group of European men in colonial attire, including one in a hat who appears to be William Penn, are interacting with a group of Native Americans. The Native Americans are dressed in traditional clothing, some with feathered headdresses. They are gathered in an outdoor setting with trees in the background and simple colonial-style houses visible behind them. To the left, a woman is seated on the ground holding a child, while other figures stand nearby. To the right, a man sits on a chest or crate, observing the exchange. The overall composition suggests a formal negotiation or treaty signing, with figures positioned to emphasize the interaction between the two groups. The style is detailed, typical of 18th or 19th-century historical book illustrations.</description></figure>\n\nThe\n\n\nEngland 25: 2: 1682\nuseful and pleasing to you. To fresh in my Love of our god\nso wonders, and I will bring you some things of our country that are\ndrunk. If anything should be out of order, apport when I come, it shall\nnor will I ever allow any of my people to get Rummo to make your people\none of my people wrong you, by good Laws I have provided for that purpose,\nwise and Loving, you may believe him. I have already taken care that none\nto him. The man which delivers this unto you, is my special friend Sober\nname, and that we who are his workmanship, may do that which is well pleasing\nGod will Incline both me and you to do. I seek nothing but the honor of his\nwe may all live in Love and peace one with another which I hope the Great\nGod brings me among you I intend to order all things in just manner that\nassure you of my love, and to desire your Love to my friends, and when the Great\nincline your hearts to righteousness love and yours. This I send to\nThe Great God, who is the power and wisdom that made you and\n\n\nYour loving freind\n\n\nI read this to the Indian\nby an interpreter in the\n6 mo 1682 Tho: Holme\n\n\n[SEALED][SIGNED]\n[SEALED_IMAGE]\nWm Penn\n\n\nOUR FATHERS WITH PEACE - LET US LOVE TO THE MOST HIGH AND BLESSED ONE [ILLUSTRATION OF A NATIVE AMERICAN AND A COLONIAL FIGURE SHAKING HANDS UNDER A SUN]\n\n\nSold by J. C. Whitney & Co., Baltimore\n\n\nMICROFILMED\n\n\n<!-- doc_id=parse-01m7hpaavnzcbs2ncvg3x1xrex -->",
